===== Run 1 =====
Comparing synchronization mechanisms for 10000000 shared increments

Mechanism | Threads | Final Count | Correctness |     Time (s)
---------------------------------------------------------------
None       |       1 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.0050
Critical   |       1 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.1182
Atomic     |       1 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.0666
Lock       |       1 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.1111
---------------------------------------------------------------
None       |       2 |      5101951 |  INCORRECT |     0.0128
Critical   |       2 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.3413
Atomic     |       2 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.1742
Lock       |       2 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.3967
---------------------------------------------------------------
None       |       4 |      2794940 |  INCORRECT |     0.0134
Critical   |       4 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.7368
Atomic     |       4 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.2035
Lock       |       4 |     10000000 |    Correct |     1.0650
---------------------------------------------------------------
None       |       8 |      3378149 |  INCORRECT |     0.0149
Critical   |       8 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.7364
Atomic     |       8 |     10000000 |    Correct |     0.2048
Lock       |       8 |     10000000 |    Correct |     1.1166
---------------------------------------------------------------

Expected correct final count = 10000000
Observation: 'None' is fastest but incorrect (race condition).
'Critical' is correct but slowest (coarse-grained locking).
'Atomic' and 'Lock' are correct; 'Atomic' scales better since it
uses lightweight hardware instructions instead of OS-level locks.

